    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Many normal faults consist of discontinuous segments that may coalesce or link across the so-called relay structures. A Boundary Element Model has been used to analyze the vertical lead to a wrong assessment of fault continuity and displacements in and around such zones. The results\r\nare compared with a model for relay evolution based on outcrop data. Several interesting observations are made, some of which have direct application to hydrocarbon entrapment and production.",
